  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 135 PM MDT Sat Aug 29 2026\n\nMonday another shortwave trough is aimed to pass over much of\nthe same areas. The monsoonal moisture should still be present \nto allow scattered showers and thunderstorms with a conditional\nrisk of severe weather and heavy rainfall. The WPC has a \nmarginal risk for excessive rainfall over much of our area with \na sliver of a slight risk into the interior basin. There is a \nshift of tails of between 0 and 1 for portions of southern \nWyoming and northwestern Colorado which could be hinting at \nrainfall overachieving. The 90th percentile of 24hr rainfall \nsouth of Rawlins is between 1 and 2 inches which makes sense \ngiven the other guidance and the outlook discussed. The rain \nshould depart east and out of the area with the shortwave and \nretreating ridge to the southeast through Tuesday.\n\nWe will be on a dry and warming trend through the rest of the\nlong term as flow aloft laxes and moisture decreases. While most \nof the upper level disturbances stay west and north, there may \nbe enough lingering monsoonal moisture to keep a few PM showers \nand thunderstorms going. Widespread rain is not expected given \nthe dry near surface conditions. Minimum afternoon RH values in \nthe mid to low 20s and gusty winds could promote elevated fire \nconditions, mainly in the northern and western counties. This \ndrying pattern is on track to be reinforced in the very \nlong term as the ridge builds back west and the jetstream stays\nout of our area.",
